# Keys trusted to sign commits, in OpenSSH allowed-signers format:
#
#   <principals> [options] <keytype> <base64-key>
#
# Git matches the *committer* email against the principals field. Only public
# keys live here, so this file is safe to publish.
#
# namespaces="git" restricts each key to signatures Git itself produced — SSH
# signatures carry a namespace, and Git uses "git".
#
# When rotating a key, don't replace the line: add the new key with
# valid-after="YYYYMMDD" and give the old one valid-before="YYYYMMDD". That
# keeps historical commits verifying instead of going bad at the swap.
#
# GitHub's merge commits are deliberately NOT listed here, and can't be. This
# file is read by `ssh-keygen -Y verify`, so every line must carry an SSH public
# key; GitHub signs with PGP, which the format has no slot for. Git notices the
# `-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----` header and hands those off to gpg regardless
# of gpg.format, so the two schemes coexist — verifying them is a keyring
# concern, not an allowed-signers one:
#
#   curl -sS https://github.com/web-flow.gpg | gpg --import
#
# That takes `git log --format=%G?` from E (can't check — no public key) to U
# (good signature, unknown validity). It stops at U rather than G because G
# additionally requires setting ownertrust on a key belonging to GitHub.
{{ .email }} namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIFO9fZ9+ST3WSFxlfTaIr1MIDLxT3OQFmsnd/Lw0zWBh
